<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad III as-Sadiq</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1813">1813</a>–<a href="/years/1882">1882</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Sadok Bey, Muhammad as-Sadiq Bey</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad III as-Sadiq was bey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husainid-dynasty">Husainid dynasty</a> from 1859 to 1882. In 1861 he promulgated a constitution, the first in the Arab world, but it was suspended in 1864 after a revolt against higher taxes. Heavy debts led to an international financial commission in 1869, and after a French invasion in 1881 he signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bardo">Treaty of Bardo</a>, accepting a French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-iii-as-sadiq.jpg" alt="Muhammad III as-Sadiq" width="320" height="489" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Louis Simil (1822-?) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sadok_Bey.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
